          Slack
        </div>
        <div class="font-display text-4xl mb-2 text-[var(--color-ink)] tracking-tight">
          ~2,000 <span class="text-[var(--color-ink-faint)] text-xl font-sans font-normal">team messages</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
          Past this threshold, retention jumps to <strong class="text-[var(--color-ink)]">93%</strong>. Below it, teams churn. The product itself doesn't change — the team's behavior does.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile rounded-xl border-l-4 border-[var(--color-accent)] border-y border-r border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-paper)] p-6">
        <div class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[var(--color-accent)] font-bold mb-3">
          Facebook
        </div>
        <div class="font-display text-4xl mb-2 text-[var(--color-ink)] tracking-tight">
          7 friends <span class="text-[var(--color-ink-faint)] text-xl font-sans font-normal">in 10 days</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] leading-relaxed">
          Chamath Palihapitiya's growth team identified this exact number. Cross it and the user retained for life. Miss it and they churned. Every product roadmap decision was filtered through it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Zunou's hypothesis — derived, not invented -->
    <p
      class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-[var(--color-ink-faint)] font-semibold mb-5"
      data-reveal="fade-up"
    >
      Zunou's hypothesis · derived from how the product creates value
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8" data-reveal="fade-up" data-reveal-delay="1">
      <Stat
        icon="users-round"
        value="5"
        label="Colleagues from the same community, active"
